PRO-FORMA RESOLUTIONS OF THE INITIAL MEMBERS AND MANAGERS OF KR YUHANGHOESA (YUHAN HOESA) ADOPTED ON [DATE] IN SEOUL, REPUBLIC OF KOREA.

WHEREAS the Company is to be formed as a Yuhan Hoesa, a limited liability company, under Chapter V-2 of the Commercial Act of the Republic of Korea, Articles 287-2 through 287-40;

WHEREAS the initial members have subscribed in full to the Company's authorized capital, have irrevocably committed to pay the respective contributions without set-off, reduction, or excuse, and have approved the final form of the formation contract and articles of incorporation;

WHEREAS the articles of incorporation require the Company to maintain a registered office in the Republic of Korea and to have at least one manager who may legally bind and represent the Company;

WHEREAS the Company needs a corporate bank account, payment processor mandates, and statutory officers to complete incorporation, business registration, and e-commerce merchant onboarding;

NOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED that:

1. SHARE CAPITAL SUBSCRIPTION AND CONTRIBUTION. The initial subscription schedule is approved. Each subscriber shall pay one hundred percent of the subscribed capital by the date set out in the formation contract into a segregated capital deposit or escrow account opened in the name of the Company with an authorized Korean commercial bank. No amount of subscribed capital shall be withdrawn before the completion of the incorporation registration. For any non-cash contribution, the managers shall obtain an independent valuation and complete transfer of title prior to filing the incorporation application.

2. BANKING AUTHORIZATION. The Company shall open a corporate operating account with a licensed Korean bank. The Managing Manager, acting jointly with the Company Secretary, is authorized to execute account opening forms, signature mandates, wire transfer instructions, and payment processor onboarding documents for Stripe and PayPal. The bank and payment processors may continue to rely on this resolution until written notice of revocation is delivered.

3. REGISTERED OFFICE ADOPTION. The address stated in the articles of incorporation is ratified, adopted, and approved as the Company's statutory registered office. The managers are directed to complete the court registry incorporation filing, the National Tax Service business registration, and all required e-commerce seller notifications, and to display the corporate location on all sales platforms.

4. STATUTORY OFFICER APPOINTMENTS. [Name] is appointed Managing Manager and Chief Executive Officer; [Name] is appointed Company Secretary; [Name] is appointed Compliance Officer and, if required, Internal Auditor. Each statutory officer shall have full authority to execute statutory instruments, sign forms, appear before Korean courts, tax authorities, and municipal agencies, and bind the Company within the scope of their offices until successors are duly appointed.

This resolution is effective immediately, shall be entered into the corporate minute book, and may be issued in counterpart by the Company Secretary on request.

Passed and approved by the initial members and managers on the date first written above.
